TASK 12 – RE-EVALUATE AND ADAPT THE DESIGN

A. Impact Analysis

The metro system introduces a fourth zone, a weekend discount and a monthly pass.

The following components require modification:

1. Zone enumeration: Add Airport.
2. Fare table: Add Airport fare of Rs 60 (6000 paise).
3. Fare calculation: Add a 10% weekend discount.
4. Peak-hour calculation: Retain the existing 20% surcharge.
5. SmartCard class: Allow journeys covered by a monthly pass.
6. MonthlyPass class: Store two selected zones and check whether a journey is covered.

B. Evaluation of Original Design

The original three-zone design cannot directly support all the new requirements without modification.

The original switch or fare-selection logic must support Airport. The fare calculation must also distinguish weekend journeys from weekday peak journeys.

The SmartCard class can be retained because its responsibility of protecting the card balance remains unchanged.

A separate MonthlyPass class is recommended because pass eligibility is different from ordinary fare calculation.

Worked examples

Example| Calculation| Result
Airport weekday off-peak| Rs 60| Rs 60
Inner weekend| Rs 10 - 10%| Rs 9
Outer weekday peak| Rs 30 + 20%| Rs 36
Middle weekend peak| Rs 20 - 10%| Rs 18

The weekend discount does not combine with the peak surcharge.

C. Adaptation or Redesign

The original design is modified rather than completely replaced.

The revised design includes:

- "enum class Zone" with four zones.
- A fare table containing four base fares.
- A separate weekend-discount rule.
- A peak-hour rule for weekdays.
- A "SmartCard" class for balance management.
- A "MonthlyPass" class for two selected zones.

The monthly pass allows unlimited journeys in its two selected zones in this simplified implementation. Journeys outside those zones are charged normally.

D. Evidence-Based Defence

The program uses the following test examples:

Test| Expected result
Inner weekend| Rs 9
Outer weekday peak| Rs 36
Middle journey with valid pass| Free
Airport weekday off-peak| Rs 60
Airport journey with only Rs 55 remaining| Denied

The program starts with Rs 100.

- After the Inner weekend journey: Rs 91.
- After the Outer peak journey: Rs 55.
- The Middle journey is covered by the monthly pass, so the balance remains Rs 55.
- The Airport journey is denied because deducting Rs 60 would leave less than Rs 50.

This demonstrates that the minimum-balance rule is still protected.

Complexity Analysis

Let n represent the number of journeys.

Operation| Time complexity
Fare lookup| O(1)
Peak-hour check| O(1)
Weekend discount calculation| O(1)
Monthly-pass zone check| O(1)
Smart-card balance check| O(1)
Processing n journeys| O(n)

The fare table and monthly-pass data use O(1) space because they store a fixed number of values.

Final Decision

I recommend modifying the original design.

The revised design retains the useful SmartCard class and integer-paise calculations while adding the Airport zone, weekend discount and MonthlyPass class.

This approach avoids unnecessary redesign, keeps the code organised and supports the changed requirements. A production system would also need pass-expiry validation, secure transactions and persistent card records.

Conclusion

The modified design supports four zones, applies the weekend discount without combining it with the peak surcharge, and provides unlimited journeys in two selected zones through a monthly pass. It preserves the minimum-balance rule for journeys paid using the stored-value card.